\begin{itemize}
\item The tax residency rules are different for each country and hence it is important to look at the individual country rules when determining the tax residency status of individuals.
\item Generally, an individual is considered a tax resident of a particular country if they meet certain criteria set by the country’s tax authority. These criteria may include factors such as physical presence in the country, days spent in the country, and citizenship status.
\item For example, in Australia, an individual is considered a tax resident if they satisfy any one of the following criteria:
\begin{itemize}
\item They are physically present in Australia for more than 183 days in a financial year;
\item Their spouse is an Australian citizen and they live in Australia for more than half of the financial year;
\item They do not have a permanent residence outside of Australia;
\item They have been a resident of Australia for more than half of the previous five financial years; or
\item They are an Australian citizen or permanent resident living abroad.
\end{itemize}
\item Similarly, in France, an individual is considered a tax resident if they have lived in France for more than 183 days in a calendar year.
\item However, in some cases, an individual may be considered a tax resident of two countries simultaneously depending on their circumstances. This is known as dual residency and can occur when an individual has ties to multiple countries through factors such as citizenship, domicile, or employment.
\item In such cases, the individual must determine which country has the primary right to tax them based on their specific situation. If both countries claim the right to tax the individual, then they may need to pay taxes in both countries.
\item To avoid double taxation, many countries have entered into tax treaties that allow individuals who are residents of both countries to be taxed only once.
\end{itemize}

